DirectoryIndex index.php

# Strato runs everything under cgi-bin/ as CGI, which 500s static files without this.
# Any new asset type must be added here or it will 500 in production while working locally.
<FilesMatch "\.(css|js|map|svg|png|jpe?g|gif|ico|webp|avif|woff2?)$">
    SetHandler default-handler
</FilesMatch>

# Compress what compresses. Measured on the live host 2026-09-05: Strato sends no Content-Encoding
# at all, so main.js arrived as 1,589 bytes of plain text, byte-identical to the file on disk. The
# whole module graph is ~50 KB raw and ~24 KB gzipped, and the stylesheet another 13 KB — so this
# block is worth more than any build-side change we can make, and costs nothing to ship.
#
# Listed by the type the server actually sends, not the type we assume: .js comes back as
# `text/javascript` here, so `application/javascript` alone would silently match nothing.
#
# Deliberately absent: png, jpe?g, gif, ico, webp, avif, woff2. Those are already compressed, and
# running DEFLATE over them spends CPU to make them very slightly larger.
#
# <IfModule> means an absent module is silence rather than a 500 — which cuts both ways, since a
# Strato that lacks mod_deflate would leave this doing nothing with no sign. Re-check with
# `curl -sI -H 'Accept-Encoding: gzip' https://neurosys.gg/assets/js/main.js | grep -i encoding`
# after deploying; a missing content-encoding line means the module is not there.
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/javascript \
                                  application/javascript application/json \
                                  application/xml image/svg+xml
</IfModule>

# Brotli beats gzip by roughly 15-20% on text and every browser that reaches this site supports it.
#
# Measured on the live host 2026-09-05: mod_brotli is NOT loaded, so this block currently does
# nothing and mod_deflate above is what answers. Kept rather than deleted — it costs nothing while
# absent and starts working the day Strato loads the module, which is worth asking them about. Do
# not read its presence as evidence it is in effect; ask the server:
#   curl -sI -H 'Accept-Encoding: br' https://neurosys.gg/assets/js/main.js | grep -i encoding
<IfModule mod_brotli.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType BROTLI_COMPRESS text/html text/plain text/css text/javascript \
                                          application/javascript application/json \
                                          application/xml image/svg+xml
</IfModule>

# Cache lifetimes, in two tiers, split by whether the URL names its own content.
#
# tools/build-assets.mjs stamps every built asset with a hash of what is in it, so the site asks for
# /assets/js/main.js?v=49d36f3e — a URL that cannot come to mean something else. Those can be kept
# for a year and never revalidated, which is what `immutable` says: do not even ask on a reload.
# The version is set by the rule further down, after RewriteEngine is on.
#
# Everything else keeps a calendar TTL, because the bare URL genuinely can change meaning under a
# cache. That is the dangerous pairing: a stale main.js against a freshly deployed document is the
# mirror drift the parity tests exist to catch, arriving by the one route no test can see — an
# element that does not know a new attribute, or a Tag case the stylesheet has moved on from. So an
# hour for anything that carries behaviour and arrives unversioned (nothing the site emits does, but
# a hand-typed or bookmarked URL would), and thirty days for images and fonts, which are vendored
# and hand-placed rather than built, and where staleness is cosmetic rather than breaking.
#
# `env=!VERSIONED` rather than trusting the order these appear in: a Header inside <FilesMatch> is
# merged after one outside it, so the short TTL would otherwise overwrite the long one. Making the
# two conditions mutually exclusive means neither depends on where it sits in the file.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    <FilesMatch "\.(css|js)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600"
    </FilesMatch>

    <FilesMatch "\.(svg|png|jpe?g|gif|ico|webp|avif|woff2?)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=2592000"
    </FilesMatch>

    <FilesMatch "\.map$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600"
    </FilesMatch>

    # Last, and matching the same files as the first block, because that is what makes it win: two
    # <FilesMatch> sections matching one file merge in document order, and `Header set` replaces.
    # Relying on the order is deliberate rather than lazy — the alternative, `env=!VERSIONED` on the
    # short tier, silently stops working the moment the rewrite below becomes an internal redirect
    # and the variable arrives named REDIRECT_VERSIONED instead.
    #
    # Both spellings are set for exactly that reason: which one Apache hands to mod_headers depends
    # on whether the rewrite changed the path, and only one of the two will ever be defined.
    <FilesMatch "\.(css|js|map)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" env=VERSIONED
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" env=REDIRECT_VERSIONED
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

RewriteEngine On
RewriteRule ^ - [E=HTTP_AUTHORIZATION:%{HTTP:Authorization}]

# Strip the build-stamp segment, and mark what it named as immutable.
#
# tools/build-assets.mjs writes /assets/js/v-a1b2c3d4/main.js into the manifest. The segment is not a
# directory — it exists so that a relative import specifier carries the version without any file
# having to be rewritten: that URL importing `./model/Tag.js` asks for
# /assets/js/v-a1b2c3d4/model/Tag.js, which lands back here and resolves to the same real file.
#
# That is what keeps the compiled JS byte-identical to tsc's output, which in turn is what lets the
# client tests import public/assets/js/model/Tag.js by name and lets coverage attribute to it. A
# `?v=` query on each specifier was tried first and cost exactly that: V8 attributes a module
# reached through a stamped specifier to a URL with the query on it, which the coverage include glob
# does not match, so the 100% gate collapsed to 68%.
#
# **Mirrored in tools/dev-router.php**, which does the same strip for the `php -S` the verify script
# runs, since the built-in server reads no .htaccess. The verify script pins that both agree.
RewriteRule ^assets/(js|css)/v-[0-9a-f]{8}/(.*)$ /assets/$1/$2 [E=VERSIONED:1,L]

# Force HTTPS, before anything else runs. Both gates are HTTP Basic and Basic is base64, so a
# request that arrives in plaintext has already put its credentials on the wire — this redirect
# fixes the next request, and the Strict-Transport-Security header stops there being a plaintext
# one at all. See src/NeuroSYS/Http/Security/StrictTransportSecurity.php.
#
# Two conditions, and both must fail before the redirect fires, because a redirect that triggers on
# a request which already arrived over HTTPS is an infinite loop. Strato terminates TLS at its
# proxy, where %{HTTPS} can read "off" on a request that was encrypted the whole way; there
# X-Forwarded-Proto is the one telling the truth. Neither is reliable alone, so ask both.
RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

# Pass real files and directories through (assets, etc.)
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d

# Everything else → router
RewriteRule ^ index.php [QSA,L]
